    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With the advent of the globalized economy, this has posed a unique threat for the health and safety of workers globally. Now that multinationals can outsource their labor to developing countries for low wages and high scalability of labor forces, there is a growing concern that the working conditions found in these overseas factories—particularly for the electronics manufacturing industry— are dangerous in occupational safety practices, resulting in high rates of injury and documented occupational diseases. Working with a prominent electronics multinational, Firm X, we collected audit data from 538 factories spanning 23 separate countries and 259 cities, the largest of its kind for the multinational. We found that out of the 525 factories that documented accidents or incidents in the past two years, 485 factories (92.38%), reported 0 of either injuries or accidents. A similar trend of little to no reporting was observed for documented abnormal occupational health (OH) exams; out of the 526 factories that documented occupational health exam evaluations, 449 of them (85.36%) reported 0 abnormalities. Overall, 80% of the audited factories reported 0 occupational injuries or abnormal occupational health exams within the past two years, which is vastly below the hypothesized results, insomuch that the credibility of these data was called into question. When compared with national averages and nongovernmental organization (NGO) audits, these data are vastly lower, strongly indicating that underreporting has taken place within Firm X’s supply chain. Thus, we believe that these data do not provide an accurate depiction of injuries rates and occupational-related illnesses for workers within Firm X’s supply chain, calling for improved modes of collecting health and safety reports through the supply chain in order to protect worker health and safety. We believe this finding is not unique to Firm X, and that understanding the working conditions in the electronics manufacturing industry, especially within developing countries, is a continual challenge that must be further investigated.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
